ition to the left (i.e., multiplied by 10). So step 2 would be 21\u00d78=168, then shifted left by one to become 1680. Then adding the first step (0) gives 1680. \n\nI think the correct way is to break it down by each digit's place value. So, for each digit in the second number (80), starting from the right:\n\n- Ones place: 0 \u2192 21 \u00d7 0 = 0\n\n- Tens place: 8 \u2192 21 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 10 (since it's the tens place) \u2192 168 \u00d710 = 1680\n\nAdding those two gives 0 + 1680 = 1680. \n\nI should present this step-by-step as per the template. Let me make sure there's no mistake. 21 times 80: 21*80 is indeed 1680. Alternatively, 20*80 = 1600 and 1*80=80, so 1600+80=1680. Yep, that checks out. \n\nSo the reasoning steps are correct. I just need to format it according to the template provided.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 21 and 80?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 80 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.\n1. Multiply 21 by the ones place digit 0 of 80:  \n   \\(21 \\times 0 = 0\\).  \n2. Multiply 21 by the tens place digit 80 of 80 (since 8 is in the tens place, it represents \\(8 \\times 10\\)):  \n   \\(21 \\times 80 = 1680\\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(0 + 1680 = 1680\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{1680}.",
