re. Maybe using the distributive property: 25 * 36 = 25*(30 +6) = 25*30 +25*6, which is exactly what I did. 25*30 is 750, 25*6 is 150, so adding them gives 900. That checks out. Alternatively, maybe multiply 25 by 36 directly. Let's see:\n\n25  \nx36  \n------  \nFirst multiply 25 by 6: 150  \nThen multiply 25 by 30 (so shift one position to the left): 750  \nAdd them: 150 + 750 = 900. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using another approach: since 25 is a quarter of 100, perhaps 36 times 25 is the same as 36 * 100 /4. 36*100 is 3600, divided by 4 is 900. That works too. So that confirms the answer is 900. \n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to the same answer, so I'm confident that the product of 25 and 36 is indeed 900.\n"]
