g 200 to 3000 gives 3200, then 50 more gives 3250. Yeah, that's right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do this another way to verify. Let me try multiplying 50 by 65 directly. 50 times 65. Since 50 is half of 100, maybe 65 times 100 is 6500, so half of that is 3250. Oh, that's another way to see it. So that confirms the same answer. That's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n   65\nx 50\n-----\n   00   (65 * 0, but since it's the tens place, actually it's shifted)\n3250    (65 * 50, which is 65*5*10=325*10=3250)\n-----\n3250\n\nSo yeah, that also gives 3250. So all methods are pointing to the same answer. Therefore, the product of 50 and 65 is 3250.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't make any calculation errors. Let me do 50*60 again. 50*60: 5*6=30, then two zeros, so 3000. Correct. 50*5=250. 3000+250 is 3250. Yes, that's right. Okay, so I think that's the correct answer. I can't see any mistakes here. So the final answer should be 3250.\n"]  6%|▌         | 29/500 [16:03<4:02:32, 30.90s/it]  6%|▌         | 30/500 [16:34<4:01:57, 30.89s/it]
