y the entire multiplication. Wait, perhaps the user's example was showing the breakdown as:\n\nFor 153 (which is 1 hundred, 5 tens, 3 ones):\n\n1. 27\u00d73 (ones)\n\n2. 27\u00d750 (tens)\n\n3. 27\u00d7100 (hundreds)\n\nThen add them all. So similarly, for 80, which is 8 tens and 0 ones, the steps would be:\n\n1. 94\u00d70 (ones place)\n\n2. 94\u00d780 (tens place)\n\nThen sum those two. So that's exactly what I did. So the answer is indeed 7520.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user expects the tens place to be treated as 8 (the digit) multiplied by 10, so 94\u00d78\u00d710. Which is the same as 94\u00d780. So the steps are correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 7520. I think that's solid. Let me just write out the steps as per the template.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 94 and 80?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 80 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps.  \n1. Multiply 94 by the ones place digit 0 of 80:  \n   \\( 94 \\times 0 = 0 \\).  \n2. Multiply 94 by the tens place digit 80 of 80:  \n   \\( 94 \\times 80 = 7,520 \\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 0 + 7,520 = 7,520 \\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **7,520**.",
