5 = 25*(60+5) which is what I did before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe think of 25*65 as 25*(70 -5). Let me see: 25*70 is 1750, minus 25*5=125. So 1750-125=1625. That also works. So same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 1625. Let me check with another approach. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication steps:\n\nMultiply 25 by 5 (units digit):\n\n25  \n×\xa065  \n─────  \n125  (this is 25×5)  \nNow multiply 25 by 6 (tens digit, which is actually 60), so shift one position to the left:  \n1500 (this is 25×60, since 25×6=150, then add a zero)  \nAdd them together:  \n125  \n+1500  \n─────  \n1625  \n\nYep, that's consistent. \n\nI think that's solid. So the answer should be 1625. Let me just do a quick estimation to check. 25 is a quarter of 100, so 65 times 100 would be 6500, divided by 4 is 1625. Exactly. So that matches. \n\nAlright, so all methods confirm that 25 multiplied by 65 is 1625. I think that's correct.\n"] 11%|█         | 56/500 [31:36<4:08:21, 33.56s/it]
