\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Maybe another way: 20 times 20 is 400, and 6 times 20 is 120, so 400 + 120 = 520. Hmm, that works too. \n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: (20 + 6) * 20 = 20*20 + 6*20 = 400 + 120 = 520. Yep, same result. \n\nI think all methods are pointing to 520. Let me just do the standard algorithm again step by step to be thorough.\n\nWrite 26  \n×20  \n________  \nFirst, multiply 26 by 0:  \n26 × 0 = 0. Write down 0.  \n\nThen multiply 26 by 2 (the tens place, so actually 20).  \n26 × 2 = 52. Since it's the tens place, we write 52 but shifted one position to the left, making it 520.  \n\nNow add the two lines:  \n0  \n+520  \n=520  \n\nYes, that's correct. I don't see any errors here. So the product should be 520.\n"]
