+ 4 (from 434) = 9\n\nTens place: 8 + 3 = 11, so write 1 and carry over 1\n\nOnes place: 0 +4 =4\n\nWait, actually, maybe I messed up the alignment. Wait, 5580 is:\n\n5 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 0 (ones)\n\n434 is:\n\n0 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 4 (ones)\n\nSo adding:\n\nThousands: 5 +0 =5\n\nHundreds:5 +4 =9\n\nTens:8+3=11, so 1 in tens place, carryover 1 to hundreds. Wait but hundreds already has 9, so adding the carryover makes hundreds 10? Hmm, perhaps better to do step by step:\n\n5580 + 434:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes: 0 +4 =4\n\nTens:8 +3 =11, so write 1, carryover 1\n\nHundreds:5 +4 =9, plus the carried 1 =10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nThousands:5 +0 =5 plus the carried 1 makes 6.\n\nSo total is 6014. Wait so 5580 +434 = 6014? Let me check with another method. 5580 + 400 is 5980, then add 34 more: 5980 +34 =6014. Yes, that's correct.\n\nTherefore the total product should be 6014. Wait, but let me verify with another method to be sure. Maybe using the standard algorithm for 62 \u00d7 97.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 62 \u00d7 97 directly as:\n\n62  \n\u00d797  \n------  \nFirst multiply 62 by 7: 434\n\nThen multiply 62 by 90 (since the 9 is in the tens place, so 9\u00d710). So 62\u00d790 = 5580. Then add them together, which is 434 +5580=6014. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, another way to check: 62 \u00d7 100 would be 6200. But 97 is 3 less than 100, so 62\u00d797 = 62\u00d7(100-3) = 62\u00d7100 - 62\u00d73 = 6200 - 186 = 6014. Yep, that matches. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore the answer is 6014. I think that's correct. Let me just go through the steps again to ensure I didn't make any calculation errors.\n\nFirst step: 62\u00d77. 60\u00d77=420, 2\u00d77=14. 420+14=434. Correct.\n\nSecond step: 62\u00d790. 62\u00d79=558, so 558\u00d710=5580. Correct.\n\nAdding 434 +5580: 434+5580. 434+5000=5434, 5434+580=6014. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, 62\u00d797 using distributive property gives the same result. So I'm confident the answer is 6014.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 62 and 97?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 97 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps.  \n1. Multiply 62 by the ones place digit (7):  \n   \\(62 \\times 7 = 434\\).  \n2. Multiply 62 by the tens place digit (90):  \n   \\(62 \\times 90 = 5,\\!580\\).  \nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(434 + 5,\\!580 = 6,\\!014\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{6014}\\).",
