s (80 + 4)(60 + 6). Expanding that, it's 80*60 + 80*6 + 4*60 + 4*6. Calculating each term:\n\n80*60 = 4800,\n\n80*6 = 480,\n\n4*60 = 240,\n\n4*6 = 24.\n\nAdding them all together: 4800 + 480 is 5280, plus 240 is 5520, plus 24 gives 5544. That matches the previous result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method for cross-verification. Let me try the standard algorithm step by step:\n\n       84\n     \u00d7 66\n     -----\n       504  (84 \u00d7 6)\n      5040  (84 \u00d7 60, shifted one position to the left)\n     -----\n      5544\n\nYes, adding those two gives 5544. \n\nWait, another way to think about it: 66 is 6 times 11, so 84 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 11. Let's see. 84 \u00d76 is 504, then 504 \u00d711. Multiplying by 11 can be done by adding the digits. Since 504 \u00d710 is 5040 plus 504 is 5544. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property in a different way. 84 \u00d766 = (80 +4)(60 +6) as before. All methods lead to the same result, so I think 5544 is the correct product. \n\nI should make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. Let me check each step again. \n\nFirst step: 84 \u00d76. 80\u00d76=480, 4\u00d76=24, 480+24=504. Correct. \n\nSecond step: 84\u00d760. 84\u00d76=504, so 84\u00d760=504\u00d710=5040. Correct. \n\nAdding 504 +5040. 5040 + 500 is 5540, plus 4 is 5544. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, 5040 + 504: 5040 + 500 is 5540, then add 4 more to get 5544. \n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use another method like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, use calculator verification, but since this is a thought process, I can't. But since all the manual calculations agree, I feel confident that 5544 is the correct product.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 84 and 66?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 66 has two digits, so we can break it into ones and tens places.  \n1. Multiply 84 by the ones place digit (6):  \n   \\( 84 \\times 6 = 504 \\).  \n