t. Like, if you have 30 boxes each containing 27 apples, how many apples total? Well, 30 times 20 is 600, 30 times 7 is 210, total 810. Makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, using exponent rules? Not sure if that applies here. Maybe not necessary. But the multiple methods all confirm the same answer, so I'm confident it's 810. \n\nWait, just to ensure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. Let's do 30\u00d727 again step by step. \n\nBreaking 27 into 20 +7:\n\n30\u00d720 = 600 (since 3\u00d72=6, and there are two zeros total from 30 and 20, so 600).\n\n30\u00d77 = 210 (3\u00d77=21, add one zero for the 30, so 210).\n\nAdding 600+210:\n\n600 + 200 = 800, plus 10 is 810. Yep, same as before. \n\nAlternatively, using the commutative property, 27\u00d730 is the same as 30\u00d727, so maybe compute 27\u00d730 directly. \n\n27\u00d710 is 270, so 27\u00d730 is three times that, which is 270\u00d73. 200\u00d73=600, 70\u00d73=210, so 600+210=810. \n\nAlright, I think that's thorough. All different methods give the same answer, so the product of 30 and 27 is definitely 810.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 30 and 27?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 27 has two digits, so we can break the multiplication into two steps.  \n1. Multiply 30 by the ones place digit (7):  \n   \\( 30 \\times 7 = 210 \\).  \n2. Multiply 30 by the tens place digit (20):  \n   \\( 30 \\times 20 = 600 \\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 210 + 600 = 810 \\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **810**.",
