final_output:  ["\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 11 and 35?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 35 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps:\n1. Multiply 11 by the ones place digit 5:  \n   \\(11 \\times 5 = 55\\).  \n2. Multiply 11 by the tens place digit 30 (since 3 in the tens place represents 30):  \n   \\(11 \\times 30 = 330\\).  \n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(55 + 330 = 385\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **385**.", "Okay, so I need to find the product of 11 and 35. Let me think about how to approach this. Hmm, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method where I break down the numbers. Since 35 is a two-digit number, I can split it into 30 and 5. That way, I can multiply 11 by each part separately and then add the results together.\n\nFirst, let me multiply 11 by 5. 11 times 5... well, 10 times 5 is 50, and 1 times 5 is 5, so adding those gives 55. Okay, that part is straightforward.\n\nNext, I need to multiply 11 by 30. Since 30 is 3 times 10, I can do 11 times 3 first. 11 times 3 is 33. Then multiply that by 10, which just adds a zero at the end, making it 330.\n\nNow, I have to add the two results together: 55 and 330. Let me add those. 55 plus 330... 55 plus 300 is 355, and then plus 30 more makes 385. Wait, no, that doesn't sound right. Wait, 55 + 330. Let me check again. 330 plus 50 is 380, and then plus 5 is 385. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to do this using the distributive property. Like, 11 times 35 is the same as (10 + 1) times 35. So that would be 10*35 + 1*35. 10 times 35 is 350, and 1 times 35 is 35. Adding those together gives 350 + 35 = 385. Oh, same result. That's good.\n\nWait, maybe I should just do the standard multiplication algorithm step by step to be thorough. Let me write it out like:\n\n\u2003\xa035\n×11\n------\n\u200335  (this is 35 × 1)\n+350 (this is 35 × 10, shifted one position to the left)\n------\n\xa0385\n\nYes, that adds up to 385. So both methods give the same answer. I think that's solid. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my head. 11 times 35. Since 10 times 35 is 350, adding another 35 gives 385. Yep, that's right. I don't see any mistakes here. So the final answer should be 385.\n"]
