t (1) and then shifting by the place value. Like, in the example, when they multiplied 27 by 50, that was actually 27×5 (the digit in tens place) and then multiplied by 10. So perhaps each step is multiplying by the digit and then accounting for the place value by adding zeros?\n\nWait, let me re-examine the example given. The original problem was 27 × 153. The reasoning steps were:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (the ones place)\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (the tens place digit 5, so 5×10)\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (the hundreds place digit 1, so 1×100)\nThen sum all those.\n\nAh, right, so each step is multiplying the first number by the digit in that place, then multiplied by the place value (1 for ones, 10 for tens, 100 for hundreds, etc.). So for 54 × 10:\n\nFirst, the ones place digit is 0. So step 1: 54 × 0 × 1 = 0.\n\nStep 2: the tens place digit is 1. So 54 × 1 × 10 = 54 ×10 = 540.\n\nThen adding those two results: 0 + 540 = 540. So the answer is 540. That makes sense. \n\nI think I was overcomplicating it by thinking of the second step as 54 ×10 directly, but actually breaking it down into the digit (1) times its place value (10) gives the correct components. So the process is valid. \n\nTherefore, following the template, the reasoning should outline each step as multiplying by the digit and then the place value. Let me structure it properly now.\n"]
