 836. Hmm, so that would be 836. But let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, 19 times 44. Maybe I can do 20 times 44 and subtract 1 times 44. Because 19 is 20 minus 1. \n\n20*44 = 880. Then subtract 1*44 which is 44. So 880 -44 = 836. Okay that matches. So that's a good check.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n   19\nx 44\n-------\nFirst multiply 19 by 4 (ones place): 76\nThen multiply 19 by 40 (tens place, so shift one position): 760\nAdd them: 76 + 760 = 836. Yep, same result.\n\nSo I think that's correct. The answer should be 836. Let me just ensure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in the steps. \n\nWait, when I first calculated 19*4, I got 76. Correct. 19*40 is indeed 760. Adding them gives 836. The alternative method also gives 836. So that seems consistent. Alright, I think that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 19 and 44?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 44 has two digits, so we can break it into two steps.  \n1. Multiply 19 by the ones place digit (4):  \n   \\(19 \\times 4 = 76\\).  \n2. Multiply 19 by the tens place digit (40):  \n   \\(19 \\times 40 = 760\\).  \nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(76 + 760 = 836\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **836**.",
