he tens place, which is 6 (since 61 is 6 in the tens place and 1 in the ones). So, we multiply 89 by 6, but since it's the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 60. \n\nCalculating 89 × 6:  \n80 ×6 = 480  \n9 ×6 =54  \nAdding them gives 480 +54 = 534. Then, since it's the tens place, we write this as 5340. Now, adding the two partial products: 89 (from the ones) and 5340 (from the tens). \n\nAdding 5340 +89:  \n5340 + 80 = 5420  \n5420 +9 = 5429. \n\nSame result. Okay, that checks out. Wait a second, but sometimes when doing multiplication, especially with larger numbers, it's easy to make an arithmetic error. Let me confirm once more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method like the lattice method or another approach to verify. Alternatively, breaking down both numbers. Let's see:\n\n89 ×61 can also be thought of as (90 -1) × (60 +1). Expanding this, using the distributive property:\n\n90×60 + 90×1 -1×60 -1×1. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90×60 = 5400  \n90×1 =90  \n-1×60 =-60  \n-1×1= -1  \n\nAdding them all together: 5400 +90 = 5490; 5490 -60 = 5430; 5430 -1 =5429. \n\nSame answer again. Hmm, so that's three different methods all leading to 5429. Seems solid. \n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me do straight multiplication:\n\n89 ×61. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can compute 89×60 first, which is 5340, then add 89. 5340 +89 is 5429. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using a calculator in my mind. Let me see: 90×60 is 5400, but since it's 89×61, which is 1 less in the first number and 1 more in the second. So, (90-1)(60+1) = 90×60 +90×1 -1×60 -1×1 = same as before. \n\nAlternatively, another way: 89 ×61. Let me think of 89×60 as (80×60) + (9×60) = 4800 +540 = 5340, then adding 89 gives 5340 +89 = 5429. \n\nAll methods converge. So I think the answer is 5429. \n\nBut just to make sure, perhaps I can do the multiplication step by step again, writing it out vertically:\n\nFirst, writing:\n\n\u2003\u200389  \n×\xa061  \n—————  \nFirst, multiply 89 by 1 (units place): 89  \nThen multiply 89 by 6 (tens place, so shifted one position to the left): 89×6 = 534, written as 5340.  \nAdding them:  \n\u2003\xa089  \n+5340  \n—————  \n5429  \n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check the answer by division. If 89×61 =5429, then 5429 ÷89 should be 61. Let me try dividing 5429 by 89. \n\nCalculating 89 ×60 =5340. Subtract that from 5429: 5429 -5340 =89. Then 89 ÷89=1. So total is 60 +1=61. Perfect, that works. \n\nAlternatively, 5429 divided by 61. Let's see, 61 × 89: 61×90=5490, minus 61 gives 5490-61=5429. Yep. \n\nAlright, so after multiple checks, I'm confident that the product of 89 and 61 is indeed 5429. I think that's solid. \n\nWait, just to be extra cautious, maybe I made an arithmetic mistake in one of the steps? Let me re-add 5340 and 89. \n\n5340 + 89: \n\n5340 + 80 is 5420, plus 9 is 5429. That's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 5340 + 89: \n\nStart from 5340, add 90 would be 5430, but since it's only 89, subtract 1: 5430 -1 =5429. Yep, same. \n\nAlright, I think that's thorough. I don't see any errors in my reasoning.\n"]
