ght complicate. Alternatively, perhaps the standard algorithm where you first multiply by the ones digit (0), write that, then multiply by the tens digit (6) and shift one place to the left, then add them up. \n\nYes, that\'s the standard method. Let me follow that approach. \n\nSo first step: 97 × 0 (the ones place) = 0. \n\nSecond step: 97 × 6 (the tens digit) but since it\'s the tens place, the result should be shifted one position to the left, so effectively multiplying by 60. So 97 × 6 = ?\n\nCalculating 97 × 6: Let\'s do 90×6=540 and 7×6=42. So 540+42=582. Then, because we\'re in the tens place, this 582 is actually 5820. \n\nThen add the two results: 0 + 5820 = 5820. \n\nSo the total product is 5820. Wait, let me check that again. Alternatively, maybe I should present the steps as:\n\n1. Multiply 97 by 0 (the ones place): 0\n2. Multiply 97 by 60 (the tens place value): 97×60. \n\nBut the user\'s example had breaking into digits, so perhaps breaking down 60 into 6 and 0, then handling each digit\'s place. So the second step would be multiplying by 6 (the tens digit) but considering the place. So maybe:\n\nStep 1: 97 × 0 = 0\n\nStep 2: 97 × 6 (tens digit) which gives 582, but since it\'s the tens place, we write it as 5820. Then adding 0 + 5820 gives 5820. \n\nAlternatively, in the example given, for 153, they mentioned multiplying by 50 (the tens place digit 50). Wait, in the example, number2 was 153, so the tens digit is 5, so the example said "Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153". So in that case, they considered the tens digit as 50 (since it\'s the tens place). So following that, here for 60, the tens digit is 6, so the tens place digit is 60? Wait, no. Wait, in the example, the tens digit was 5, so they multiplied by 50 (since it\'s the tens place). So for 60, the tens digit is 6, so that would be 6×10 = 60. So the second step would be multiplying by 60? Wait, but in the example, they said "Multiply number1 27 by the tens place d