\nThen multiply 75 by 1 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 10), so 75 × 10 = 750.  \nAdding those two gives 375 + 750 = 1125. Yep, same result. \n\nWait, maybe another method? Using distributive property: 75 × 15 = 75 × (10 + 5) = 75×10 + 75×5 = 750 + 375 = 1125. Yep, that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of 15 as half of 30, so 75 × 30 would be 2250, then half of that is 1125. That works too. \n\nHmm, all methods lead to 1125. So I think that's correct. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 75 times 10 is 750, plus 75 times 5 is 375, so 750 + 375 is indeed 1125. Okay, that's solid. \n\nI guess the steps in the reasoning should follow the standard multiplication breakdown into ones and tens, then sum. So the answer is 1125.\n"]
