heck another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, 40 multiplied by 46 can be thought of as 40×(40 + 6) = 40×40 + 40×6 = 1600 + 240 = 1840. Yeah, that matches. But maybe there's another way to verify. Let's do the standard multiplication:\n\n   46\n× 40\n-----\n   00 (since multiplying by the 0 in 40's ones place, but shifted)\n+1840 (multiplying by the 4 in the tens place, which is actually 4×10, so 46×4=184, then add a zero)\n-----\n  1840\n\nYep, that works. So the answer should be 1840. Wait, but just to make sure I didn't mix up the steps. Let me go through the reasoning again as per the template. The example had three steps because the second number had three digits. Here, since 46 is two digits, we have two steps. So step 1 is multiplying by the ones digit (6), step 2 is multiplying by the tens digit (40). So 40×6 is 240, 40×40 is 1600. Adding them gives 1840. That seems correct. \n\nAnother way: 40×46 is the same as (4×10) × (4×10 + 6) = 4×10×4×10 + 4×10×6 = 16×100 + 24×10 = 1600 + 240 = 1840. Yep, same result. Okay, I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes here. The key is breaking it down into place values and then adding the partial products. That's the standard algorithm for multiplication. So the answer is definitely 1840.\n"]
