final_output:  ['', "Okay, so I need to find the product of 86 and 13. Let me think about how to approach this. Since 13 is a two-digit number, I can break it down into its tens and ones places. That means 13 is 10 plus 3. \n\nFirst, I'll multiply 86 by the ones digit, which is 3. Let me do that: 86 times 3. Hmm, 80 times 3 is 240, and 6 times 3 is 18. Adding those together gives 240 + 18 = 258. So that's the first part.\n\nNext, I need to multiply 86 by the tens digit. But wait, the tens digit is 1, right? Since 13 has a 1 in the tens place. But since it's the tens place, it's actually 10. So I should multiply 86 by 10. That's straightforward because multiplying by 10 just adds a zero at the end. So 86 times 10 is 860.\n\nNow, I have to add the two results together. The first step was 258 and the second was 860. Adding those: 258 + 860. Let's see, 200 + 800 is 1000, then 58 + 60 is 118. Adding those gives 1000 + 118 = 1118. Wait a second, that doesn't seem right. Let me check my addition again. 258 plus 860. Maybe breaking it down differently: 258 + 800 is 1058, then add the remaining 60? Wait no, 860 is 800 + 60. Wait, no, 860 is 800 plus 60? No, 800 plus 60 would be 860, yes. So adding 258 + 800 first gives 1058, then add 60 more: 1058 + 60 = 1118. Hmm, okay that's correct. \n\nBut let me verify the multiplication another way to be sure. Maybe using the standard algorithm. \n\nMultiplying 86 by 13:\n\nFirst, write it down:\n\n\u2003\xa086  \n×\xa013  \n─────  \nFirst multiply 86 by 3 (the ones place): 86×3=258.  \nThen multiply 86 by 10 (the tens place, so shift one position to the left): 86×10=860.  \nAdding those two results: 258 + 860 = 1118. \n\nYes, that matches. Alternatively, maybe use the distributive property: (80 + 6)(10 + 3) = 80×10 + 80×3 + 6×10 + 6×3. Let's compute each term:\n\n80×10 = 800  \n80×3 = 240  \n6×10 = 60  \n6×3 = 18  \n\nAdding all those: 800 + 240 = 1040; 1040 + 60 = 1100; 1100 + 18 = 1118. Yep, same result. \n\nSo I think the answer is 1118. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 86 times 10 is 860, plus 86 times 3 is 258, totaling 1118. Yep, that's correct. I don't see any mistakes here.\n"]
