r instance, 60 is 6\u00d710, so 39\u00d76\u00d710. \n\nCalculating 39\u00d76 first: 39\u00d76. Let me compute that. 30\u00d76=180, 9\u00d76=54, so total is 180+54=234. Then multiply by 10 gives 2340. \n\nBut that's another approach, but maybe the template wants the step-by-step based on place value digits. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem wants to use the distributive property: 39\u00d760 =39\u00d7(6\u00d710)= (39\u00d76) \u00d710. But again, the steps would be similar. \n\nGiven the example provided, the structure is breaking down the second number into its digits multiplied by their place values, then multiplying each by the first number and summing. \n\nSo in this case, since 60 is 6\u00d710 +0\u00d71, the steps are:\n\n1. 39\u00d7(0\u00d71) =0\n\n2. 39\u00d7(6\u00d710)=2340\n\nSum them: 0+2340=2340.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 2340. \n\nI think that's the way to go. Let me check if there's any mistake here. 39\u00d760 is indeed 2340. \n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. 39\u00d760. If I do 40\u00d760=2400, then subtract 1\u00d760=60, so 2400-60=2340. Yes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, 39\u00d760 = (40-1)\u00d760 =40\u00d760 -1\u00d760 =2400-60=2340. Correct. \n\nSo all methods lead to the same answer, so that's good. \n\nTherefore, following the template structure, the reasoning should be as outlined.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 39 and 60?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 60 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.\n1. Multiply 39 by the ones place digit 0 of 60:  \n   \\(39 \\times 0 = 0\\).\n2. Multiply 39 by the tens place digit 60 (since 6 is in the tens place, its value is \\(6 \\times 10 = 60\\)):  \n   \\(39 \\times 60 = 2,\\!340\\).\n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(0 + 2,\\!340 = 2,\\!340\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{2340}\\).",
