t (6), then shift it one place (multiply by 10). So maybe the steps are:\n\nStep1: 39 ×0 (the ones digit) =0\n\nStep2: 39 ×6 (the tens digit) = 234, then since it\'s the tens place, we need to multiply by 10, so 234×10=2340. \n\nBut in the example, they didn\'t write it as shifting but rather as multiplying by the place value. Let me check the example again. The example\'s second step was 27 ×50 (the 5 in the tens place, so 5×10=50). So they directly multiplied by 50. So following that, the second step here would be 39 ×60. \n\nTherefore, the two steps would be:\n\n1. 39 ×0 =0\n\n2. 39 ×60 = 2340\n\nAdding them together: 0 +2340 =2340. So the final answer is 2340. \n\nBut wait, that seems a bit redundant because the second step is actually the entire problem. Maybe the user wants to break it down more into the multiplication steps. Alternatively, perhaps the problem expects breaking down 60 into 6 and 0, so the second step is multiplying by 6 (the digit) and then shifting. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects the standard algorithm steps. Let me think again. Let me consider the standard way of multiplying 39 by 60. \n\nThe standard way is to write:\n\n\u2003\u200339  \n×\u202f60  \n————  \nFirst, multiply 39 by 0 (the ones place): 0  \nThen multiply 39 by 6 (the tens place), but since it\'s the tens place, you shift one position to the left, so it\'s 39×6=234, then write that as 2340. Then add them together: 0 +2340 =2340. \n\nSo that\'s exactly the same as the steps above. \n\nTherefore, following the template\'s example, the reasoning steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 39 by the ones place digit (0): 39×0=0\n\n2. Multiply 39 by the tens place digit (60): 39×60=2340\n\nWait, but in the example, they mentioned "Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153". So here, the tens place digit\'s value is 60, so the second step is 39×60. \n\nTherefore, the answer is the sum of 0 and 2340, which is 2340. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user expects to compute 39×6 first, then append a zero. Let me see if that\'s the case. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem wants to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. Let me think if there\'s another way. For instance, 60 is 6×10, so 39×6×10. \n\nCalculating 39×6 first: 39×6. Let me compute that. 30×6=180, 9×6=54, so total is 180+54=234. Then multiply by 10 gives 2340. \n\nBut that\'s another approach, but maybe the template wants the step-by-step based on place value digits. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem wants to use the distributive property: 39×60 =39×(6×10)= (39×6) ×10. But again, the steps would be similar. \n\nGiven the example provided, the structure is breaking down the second number into its digits multiplied by their place values, then multiplying each by the first number and summing. \n\nSo in this case, since 60 is 6×10 +0×1, the steps are:\n\n1. 39×(0×1) =0\n\n2. 39×(6×10)=2340\n\nSum them: 0+2340=2340.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 2340. \n\nI think that\'s the way to go. Let me check if there\'s any mistake here. 39×60 is indeed 2340. \n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. 39×60. If I do 40×60=2400, then subtract 1×60=60, so 2400-60=2340. Yes, that\'s correct. \n\nAlternatively, 39×60 = (40-1)×60 =40×60 -1×60 =2400-60=2340. Correct. \n\nSo all methods lead to the same answer, so that\'s good. \n\nTherefore, following the template structure, the reasoning should be as outlined.\n'] 61%|██████    | 305/500 [2:48:23<2:07:34, 39.25s/it] 61%|██████    | 306/500 [2:48:57<2:01:11, 37.48s/it]
