it (1) and then considering the place value. So written as 87 \u00d7 1 \u00d7 10. \n\nEither way, the answer is 870. Let me just confirm with another method. 87 \u00d7 10 is the same as 87 \u00d7 (10), which is 870. Yep, that's correct. \n\nI think that's all. The key points here are understanding place value and how multiplying by a digit in the tens place requires an extra zero. Since the second number here is 10, it's a simple case but important to show the steps properly as per the template.\n</think>\n\n",
