0: 5 times 7 is 35, and then add two zeros (one from 50 and one from 70), so 3500. Yeah, that's right.\n\nNow, since 73 is only two digits, there are no more steps. So we add the two results together: 150 (from step 1) plus 3500 (from step 2). Adding those gives 150 + 3500 = 3650. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a quicker way since one of the numbers is a multiple of 10. Let me check that method too. Since 50 is 5×10, multiplying 73 by 5 first and then by 10. 73×5 is 365, then times 10 is 3650. Yep, same answer. So that's a good cross-verification.\n\nWait, but the template example broke down each digit's place value. So in step 2, instead of multiplying by the tens digit (7) as 70, maybe they considered the place value. Let me confirm the steps again.\n\nOriginal example with 27×153: they did 27×3 (ones), 27×50 (tens), and 27×100 (hundreds). So for 50×73, the tens digit is 7, so that's 70, so step 2 is 50×70. So the steps are correct.\n\nAdding them up gives 150 + 3500 = 3650. That seems right. Let me just do the multiplication directly to confirm:\n\n50 × 73. \n\nMultiplying 73 by 50: since 50 is half of 100, 73×100 is 7300, so half of that is 3650. Yep, that's another way to see it. So all methods lead to the same result. Therefore, the answer is 3650.\n"] 67%|██████▋   | 333/500 [3:04:27<1:43:56, 37.34s/it] 67%|██████▋   | 334/500 [3:05:09<1:47:14, 38.76s/it]
