s here. Let me just confirm once more with another approach. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 85 into 100 - 15? Wait, 85 is 85, so maybe that's more complicated. Alternatively, 40 times 85 is the same as 85 times 40, which is commutative. Either way, the result should be the same. \n\nAnother way: 40 multiplied by 85. Let me do the standard multiplication:\n\n   85\nx 40\n-----\n   00 (since 0 times 85 is 0, but shifted)\n+3400 (since 4 times 85 is 340, then times 10 because it's the tens place)\n-----\n 3400\n\nYes, exactly. So that's the same as the previous steps. Alright, I think the answer is correct. So the reasoning steps as per the template would be:\n\n1. Multiply 40 by 5 (the ones place of 85) to get 200.\n\n2. Multiply 40 by 80 (the tens place of 85) to get 3200.\n\nAdding them gives 200 + 3200 = 3400. \n\nTherefore, the final product is 3400. That seems solid.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 40 and 85?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 85 has two digits, so we can break it into ones and tens places.  \n1. Multiply 40 by the ones place digit (5):  \n   \\( 40 \\times 5 = 200 \\).  \n2. Multiply 40 by the tens place digit (80):  \n   \\( 40 \\times 80 = 3200 \\).  \nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 200 + 3200 = 3400 \\).  \nSo, the final computed product is **3400**.",
