 correct. So the total is 832.\n\nWait, but let me confirm using another method to be sure. Let\'s do 64 ×13. Alternatively, 13 is 10 +3, so 64×10=640, 64×3=192, sum them to get 832. That\'s the same as before. Okay, so that\'s correct.\n\nSo the steps in the template need to be written out as per the instructions. Let me structure the reasoning accordingly. The problem has two digits, so two steps. The first step is multiplying by the ones digit (3), the second by the tens digit (10). Then sum them. \n\nWait, in the original example, the tens place was written as 50. So for 13, the tens digit is 1, so the tens place is 1×10, so the second step is multiplying by 10. So in the reasoning, step 2 should be "Multiply 64 by the tens place digit 10 of 13". \n\nThen the sum would be 192 +640 = 832. So the answer is 832. \n\nI think that\'s all. Let me make sure there\'s no mistake in the steps. The key is breaking down the multiplication into each place value and then adding. The user probably wants the detailed steps as in the example. Alright, that should work.\n']
