 steps. Let me check 13 times 90 again. 13 times 9 is 117, so 13 times 90 is 1170. That's correct. And 13 times 2 is definitely 26. So adding them gives 1170 +26 = 1196. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property another way. 13 times 92 is the same as (10 +3) times (90 +2). Let's expand that:\n\n10*90 + 10*2 + 3*90 + 3*2. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n10*90 = 900,\n\n10*2 = 20,\n\n3*90 = 270,\n\n3*2 =6.\n\nAdding them all together: 900 +20 is 920, plus 270 is 1190, plus 6 is 1196. Yep, same result. Okay, so that confirms it again. \n\nI think I'm confident that 13 multiplied by 92 is 1196. Let me just do a quick estimate to check if that's reasonable. 13 times 90 is 1170, and 13 more would be 1183, but since it's 92, which is 90 +2, so 1170 +26 = 1196. The estimate seems okay. Alternatively, 10 times 90 is 900, so 13 is a bit more, so 1196 sounds right. \n\nHmm, another way: 92 times 10 is 920, so 920 + (92*3). Let's see, 92*3 is 276. So 920 +276 is 1196. Exactly. That works too. \n\nAlright, so all methods lead to 1196. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. The answer should be 1196.\n</think>\n\n",
