three digits? Wait no, 60 is two digits. So the places are ones and tens. So step 1: multiply by ones digit (0), step 2: multiply by tens digit (6) but as 60. Wait, actually, maybe the way to do it is:\n\nFirst, multiply 11 by the ones digit (0) giving 0. Then multiply 11 by the tens digit (6) but since it\'s the tens place, you have to multiply by 6*10, so 60. Wait, but that\'s the same as the original problem. That can\'t be right. Maybe I need to separate the digits properly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the standard algorithm is to do:\n\n11 × 60 can be thought of as 11 × 6 × 10. Since 60 is 6×10. So 11×6 is 66, then times 10 is 660. But following the step-by-step breakdown as per the template, which splits each digit\'s contribution.\n\nAlternatively, let me try breaking down 60 into 60 + 0, but that\'s not helpful. Wait, the example\'s reasoning was:\n\nFor 27 × 153:\n\n1. 27 × 3 (ones place)\n2. 27 × 50 (tens place)\n3. 27 × 100 (hundreds place)\n\nThen add all three. So for 11 × 60, which is two digits:\n\n1. 11 × 0 (ones place)\n2. 11 × 60 (tens place, since the tens digit is 6, so 6×10=60)\n\nWait, but that would mean step 2 is 11×60, which is the original problem. That doesn\'t make sense. Maybe I need to adjust. Perhaps when the digit is in the tens place, you multiply the number by the digit times 10. So for the tens place digit (6), it\'s 6×10, so multiply 11 by 6, then by 10. Wait, but that\'s 11×6×10, which is the same as 11×60. Hmm, but how does that split into steps?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the steps are:\n\n1. Multiply 11 by the ones digit (0) → 0\n2. Multiply 11 by the tens digit (6) → 66, but since it\'s the tens place, shift one position (equivalent to multiplying by 10), so 66 ×10 = 660\n\nThen add the two results: 0 + 660 = 660. That works. So in the reasoning steps, it\'s important to mention shifting the second result by one decimal place (multiplying by 10). The example with 153 had three steps, each corresponding to a digit\'s place (units, 