sands place) plus the carried over 1 makes 5. \n\nWait, actually, maybe I messed up the alignment. Let me line them up properly:\n\n4620\n+\xa0462\n=?\n\nIt should be:\n\n4620\n+\xa0 462\n------\nAdding the ones place: 0 + 2 = 2\n\nTens place: 2 + 6 = 8\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 4 = 10, so write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 4 + 0 (since 462 has no thousands) plus the carried 1 = 5.\n\nSo the total is 5082. That seems correct. \n\nWait another way to check: Maybe using another method like the lattice method or another approach to confirm?\n\nAlternatively, I can multiply 77 by 66 directly:\n\n     77\nx\xa0  66\n--------\nFirst multiply 77 by 6 (units place): 462\n\nThen multiply 77 by 60 (tens place, so shifted one position to the left): 4620\n\nAdding them gives 462 + 4620 = 5082. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the formula (a + b)(c + d) but not sure. Alternatively, 77*66 can be thought as (70 +7)*(60+6). But that might complicate more. Alternatively, 77*66 = (70*60) + (70*6) + (7*60) + (7*6). Let me compute each term:\n\n70*60 = 4200\n\n70*6 = 420\n\n7*60 = 420\n\n7*6 = 42\n\nAdding those together: 4200 + 420 is 4620, plus another 420 is 5040, plus 42 is 5082. Same result. Okay, so that confirms it again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 77*66 as (70+7)*(66). Wait, that's the same as before. Alternatively, 66*70 is 4620, plus 66*7 is 462. Yep, same as before. So I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 77*66 is (75 +2)*(65 +1), but that might not help. Alternatively, think of 77*66 as 77*(70 -4) but that complicates more.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the difference of squares? Not sure, since 77 and 66 aren't symmetric around a common number. Maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, just do straight multiplication:\n\n77\n\ntimes 66\n\nMultiply 7 (units) by 6 (units): 42, write down 2, carryover 4.\n\n7*6 (tens place) is 42 plus the 4 is 46. So first partial product is 462.\n\nThen multiply by the tens digit of 66, which is 6 (tens place, so actually 60). So 77 * 60:\n\n7*6 is 42, but since it's the tens place, it's 420. Then 70*6 is 420, so adding gives 420 + 4200? Wait no, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit (6 in the tens place, so 60), you shift one position.\n\nSo 77 * 6 (tens place) would be 77*6 = 462, then shifted one left (multiply by 10) gives 4620.\n\nAdding 462 and 4620 gives 5082. Yep, same result. So I think the answer is 5082. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. Let me check with a calculator maybe? Wait, since I can't actually use a calculator, but let me do another verification.\n\nLet me compute 77*60 = 4620, and 77*6 = 462. Adding them together: 4620 + 462. Let me break it down again:\n\n4620 + 400 = 5020\n\n5020 + 62 = 5082. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, 66 is 6*11, so 77*6*11. 77*6 is 462, then 462*11. Hmm, 462*11 is 462*(10+1) = 4620 + 462 = 5082. That works too. So that's another way to see it. So, definitely 5082.\n\nI think that's solid. All methods lead to the same result, so I'm confident that the product of 77 and 66 is 5082.\n"]
